WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:08.241
If you could travel faster
than the speed of light,

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:09.476
where would you go?

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:21.588
This is WHAT IF,

00:00:21.588 --> 00:00:22.922
and here's what would happen

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:25.358
if we had working warp drive technology.

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:27.894
For all we know,

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:30.730
nothing can travel faster than light.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:33.333
Lightspeed seems to be
the universal speed limit

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:35.935
for everything that's moving in space.

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:38.071
Unless we build a spaceship

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:40.440
capable of distorting space-time.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:44.010
A spaceship equipped with warp drive.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.813
Our craft would be encased by a large ring

00:00:46.813 --> 00:00:49.616
that would compress space-time
in front of the spaceship

00:00:49.616 --> 00:00:52.185
and expand it to the rear of it.

00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:55.488
It wouldn't be the spacecraft
moving over the cosmic speed limit.

00:00:55.488 --> 00:01:00.260
It would be space-time itself
moving around the spacecraft.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.662
Once we figured out this
method of propulsion,

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:05.532
we could start our cosmic conquest.

00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:08.802
How far into the vastness of
the Universe would we make it?

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.672
If the warp drive could
accelerate our spacecraft

00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:16.076
to the speed of 10 times
faster than the speed of light,

00:01:16.076 --> 00:01:21.281
it would only take us about
75 seconds to get to Mars.

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:24.150
It would take even less time
to get from the Earth to the Sun -

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:26.719
some 50 seconds of space travel

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:30.223
and there's our star right in front of our eyes.

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:33.560
From there, we'd speed to the
inner limits of the Oort Cloud -

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.262
the beginning of the
edge of our Solar System.

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:38.331
At 10 times the speed of light,

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:41.101
we'd get there in about 72 days.

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:43.703
After 83 more days in space,

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:45.805
we'd arrive at Alpha Centauri -

00:01:45.805 --> 00:01:49.742
a star system that hosts the
potentially habitable exoplanet - 

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:52.112
Proxima Centauri.

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:54.147
Our very first interstellar colony

00:01:54.147 --> 00:01:56.516
would settle on Proxima Centauri,

00:01:56.516 --> 00:01:59.185
but humanity won't stop exploring.

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:03.523
Our next stop would be the Gliese 667 Cc -

00:02:03.523 --> 00:02:05.358
the hotter and bigger version of Earth 

00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:07.927
about 23.5 light-years away.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:12.398
This exoplanet might not turn
out to be too hospitable,

00:02:12.398 --> 00:02:14.000
but it could be worth sending a crew

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.270
on the 4.5-year roundtrip to check it out.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.806
Other manned crafts
would take on trips

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:24.010
to Kepler-442b and Kepler-452b -

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.211
the next exoplanets

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:27.847
on our list of potentially habitable worlds.

00:02:28.715 --> 00:02:31.417
Those spacecraft would
have to speed up a little -

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:34.354
unless they wanted to spend
over 120 years in space

00:02:34.354 --> 00:02:37.490
covering a distance of over 1,200 light-years.

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:41.594
At a speed of one thousand times
faster than the speed of light,

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:44.097
they'd only need about 437 days

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:46.666
to reach those Kepler exoplanets.

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:49.135
Even when surpassing the lightspeed limit,

00:02:49.135 --> 00:02:52.305
we'd still have to spend
years traveling across space;

00:02:52.305 --> 00:02:54.207
searching out other habitable worlds

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:55.408
and their inhabitants,

00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:56.408
if there are any.

00:02:58.906 --> 00:03:00.747
The Universe is big.

00:03:00.747 --> 00:03:02.015
Very big.

00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:03.983
And it keeps expanding.

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:06.386
We'd never be able to
travel to the edge of it,

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:08.788
even with working warp drive. 

00:03:08.788 --> 00:03:10.729
Our home galaxy, the Milky Way,

00:03:10.729 --> 00:03:13.893
is about 200,000 light-years across.

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:17.697
Even if we could achieve the
speed of one light-year per hour,

00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:19.642
it would take us 22 years

00:03:19.642 --> 00:03:23.603
to reach the outmost corner of our own galaxy.

00:03:23.603 --> 00:03:25.838
Let's come back down
to Earth for a moment.

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:28.641
The fastest space probe
we've ever come up with

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:30.877
is 90 million times slower

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:34.013
than our hypothesized lightspeed spacecraft.

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:35.381
We've got a long way to go

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:38.585
to achieve a Star Trek
level of interstellar travel.

00:03:38.585 --> 00:03:41.888
And don't forget that using
warp drive technology

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:45.825
would require an enormous
amount of energy in the first place.

00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:48.361
Maybe we should start
investing our resources

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:50.997
into building a Dyson
sphere around the Sun

00:03:50.997 --> 00:03:54.601
to fill all our energy demands once and for all.

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:58.037
But that's a story for another WHAT IF.

